Core word: "{a}". Candidate word: "{b}". Both are English words as the ESV Bible uses them.
How close is the meaning of the candidate to the core word? Score 0 to 10:
10 = the candidate means the same as the core word (in some verse one could stand for the other), also in another word form (verb, noun, adjective);
8 = nearly the same, a little milder or stronger;
5 = the same area, but a different thing (a cause, result, companion or part of it);
2 = only loosely related;
0 = unrelated, or the opposite.
Answer JSON only: {{"score":0}}

You build a collection of English words that share ONE core meaning, for a study of how Scripture describes the human inner being (words as the ESV Bible uses them).
The collection so far (core word first; forms in the text in brackets):
{members}

Candidate word: "{b}" (forms in the text: {bsf})

Does the candidate belong in this collection? It belongs ONLY if its core meaning is one of:
- synonym: means the same or nearly the same as one of the words in the collection;
- opposite: is the direct contrary of one of the words in the collection (the same matter, the other pole);
- associated: names the same matter seen from another side, so closely tied to the core meaning that a reader would file it under this same collection (not merely a word that often occurs near it).
Otherwise answer no. When unsure, answer no.
Answer JSON only: {{"relation":"synonym|opposite|associated|no","reason":null}}
"reason" only when relation is associated or opposite, under 10 words (else null).
